# bac2food — what must NOT go to a public repository.
#
# The software here is small: ~10 MB across ~90 files. Everything else in this working
# directory is data, and it is excluded for one of three reasons, in order of severity.
#
# 1. LICENCE. Several source databases may not be redistributed, and one may not be
#    redistributed at all. Committing raw source files would breach those terms.
# 2. PRIVACY. The cohort annotations carry the source cohort's sample identifiers in
#    their filenames, and the published work anonymizes those.
# 3. SIZE. GitHub rejects any file over 100 MB; chebi.obo (248 MB) and the BRENDA
#    extract (117 MB) both exceed it.
#
# Rule of thumb: parsers and pipeline code are tracked, the tables they read are not.
# Every excluded input is fetched by, or documented in, the readme of its own folder.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. licensed data
# Raw national/regional composition tables. These are the providers' own files, under
# eleven different licences (Supplementary Table S1). One permits use "only unchanged",
# which makes any derived value an amendment its terms withhold; it must never be
# published here, and neither may the provider's own file.
food_DBs/**/*.xlsx
food_DBs/**/*.xls
food_DBs/**/*.ods
food_DBs/**/*.zip
food_DBs/**/*.csv
food_DBs/**/*.tsv
food_DBs/**/*.json
food_DBs/**/*.mdb
food_DBs/**/*.accdb
food_DBs/canadian_nutrientfile_canada/NUTRIENT*
# Contents, not the directory itself: git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, and that silently swallowed this folder's SOURCE.md.
food_DBs/fooddatacentral_usa/*
# The parsers and merge scripts in those folders ARE the software — keep them.
!food_DBs/**/*.py
!food_DBs/**/readme*
# And so is the provenance: SOURCES.tsv says where each provider's file is obtained, and
# the per-folder SOURCE.md stubs generated from it are the only thing standing in for the
# releases these rules exclude. `food_DBs/**/*.tsv` above would otherwise swallow the table.
!food_DBs/SOURCES.tsv
!food_DBs/**/SOURCE.md
!food_DBs/**/README*

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- 2. cohort data
# Sample labels were anonymized to B_1..B_41 on 2026-08-05, so what is left to exclude is
# bulk and third-party ownership rather than identity. Patterns are written by TIMEPOINT,
# never by the old cohort prefix: a .gitignore is itself published, so naming the prefix
# here would leak the string being withheld.
#   - gene_annot/         110 metagenome annotation files, 365 MB, produced elsewhere
#   - *_1_month* etc.     per-sample predictor outputs and their zips, anywhere in the tree
#
# Do NOT add a bare `*_ec.tsv` rule here. It reads as "cohort EC tables", but it also
# matches 0_building/3_nutrient_to_ec.tsv — the core nutrient↔EC map that every scoring
# script reads. Excluding it ships a repository that cannot run. gene_annot/ already
# covers the cohort tables, so the rule buys nothing.
gene_annot/
*_1_month*
*_6_months*
*_12_months*

# --------------------------------------------------------------- 3. oversize inputs
# Third-party downloads, and most of them also exceed GitHub's 100 MB hard limit:
# chebi.obo/chebi.json.gz from the EBI, ec_species_substrate from BRENDA.
#
# brenda_2026_1.{json,txt}.tar.gz are the official BRENDA release files (154 MB), and
# BRENDA's own README says plainly: "The full BRENDA contents are copyright-protected."
# What this repository ships instead is the DERIVED digest (eggnog/2_digest_*.tsv), which
# is what the pipeline actually reads. Download the release yourself from
# https://www.brenda-enzymes.org/download.php if you need to rebuild the digest;
# eggnog/readme.txt gives the two commands.
chebi/chebi.obo
chebi/chebi.json.gz
chebi/rheaDB/
eggnog/*.tar.xz
eggnog/*.tar.gz
brenda/*.tar.gz
brenda/*.tar.xz

# A saved copy of BRENDA's download page, with its bundled CSS and JS. Third-party assets
# that document nothing the readme does not.
brenda/*.html
brenda/*_files/

# Providers' own conditions-of-use documents. They are the providers' documents, not
# ours to redistribute, and each provider publishes its current terms itself.
food_DBs/**/*.pdf

# ------------------------------------------------------- derived / regenerable state
# Parquet stores and caches are rebuilt by the pipeline from the inputs above; none is
# an artifact a reader needs. The deposited resource is the four TSVs on Zenodo.
#
# The packaged deposit lives at /data/bac2food/deposit/ -- four .xz files plus SHA256SUMS,
# compressed per file rather than as one tarball so a user can take licences.tsv without
# pulling 1.8 GB of species data. It is not committed because it is 70 MB of derived data
# that Zenodo versions; the repository ships the code that builds it, never the table.
# The rule below stays for the retired 2026-08-07 tarball, in case one is ever built there
# again.
*.parquet
*_bucketed/
exports/
exports_*/
5_export/exports.tar.xz

# ------------------------------------------------- superseded example outputs
# Sample results from runs of March and April 2026, kept locally as a record. They must
# not ship: their fdc_ids come from the retired offset keying (values above 51,000,000,
# e.g. 93,060,118), which no longer exists anywhere in the released resource. A reader
# joining them against the deposit would match nothing and have no way to know why.
# Regenerate them from the current tables instead; both scripts take one command.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- environments, cruft
# ec2food_env is a checked-in virtualenv (288 MB). Requirements belong in a
# requirements.txt; the tree itself never does.
ec2food_env/
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.Rhistory
.RData
*.swp
*.bak
.DS_Store

# NOTE, deliberately NOT ignored: 0_building/novel_nutrients/novel_*.csv. Those list only
# the component NAMES and units each source declares, not its values, so a user holding
# their own licensed copy can rebuild those rows. The one file of that set belonging to
# the non-redistributable source is excluded below with the rest of its material.

# ------------------------------------------- retired pipeline stages (untracked 2026-08-17)
# 1_query, 2_differential and 3_magmapper were earlier, narrower variants of the scoring
# kernel that 4_predict supersedes. They were removed from the repository, but `git rm`
# leaves a working tree alone: the directories still exist locally and still hold files
# that must never be published. Ignoring each directory whole is what keeps them out.
#
#   - superseded example outputs whose fdc_ids come from the retired offset keying
#     (values above 51,000,000, e.g. 93,060,118); they join against nothing in the release
#   - the differential-diet test outputs, whose FILENAMES carry the withheld cohort string
#   - a 288 MB checked-in virtualenv, the boss_matrices working set, and the sample list
#
# Do not narrow these to file patterns. A pattern that misses one file publishes it.
1_query/
2_differential/
3_magmapper/

# ------------------------------------------------ NEVO: permission granted 2026-08-18
# RIVM answered yes, so the local exclusions that hid this source from 2026-08-17 to
# 2026-08-18 are gone from .git/info/exclude and its code is tracked again. Nothing new is
# needed here: the raw release and the conditions PDF are already covered by the blanket
# *.xlsx / *.zip / *.pdf rules for food_DBs above, which is where they must stay -- RIVM
# asked that users be sent to their download page rather than handed the file.
#
# The reasoning that put those patterns in .git/info/exclude rather than here still stands
# for the next source in that position: this file is published, so a pattern spelling a
# withheld provider's name leaks the very string being withheld.

# In-place edit backups (bac2food_predict.py.pre_round17 and friends). These are
# near-identical copies of a 550 KB source file; two of them nearly went into a
# commit. Kept outside the repo in /data/bac2food/code_backups/ instead.
*.pre_*

# Scratch predictor configs (_tmp_params_nofiber.yaml, _tmp_params_pruned.yaml).
# They must live beside parameters.yaml -- _resolve_cfg_path resolves relative
# entries against the config's own directory -- so they land inside the repo and
# would otherwise be committed. The ablation chain writes and deletes them.
_tmp_params_*.yaml
